<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chagatai Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1227">1227</a>–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chagatai Khan was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who received a share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and ruled from 1227 to 1242. His territories included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a> valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, formerly ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>, or Chagatai Khanate, was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan.jpg" alt="Chagatai Khan" width="268" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enerelt at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_Khan_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chagatai was born in late 1183 or 1184 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khongirad">Onggirat</a> tribe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, the later Genghis Khan. Because Börte had been kidnapped by the Merkit tribe before the birth of her elder son Jochi, whose paternity was never known, Chagatai was the first son definitively fathered by Temüjin. When Genghis divided his new nation after the assembly of 1206, Chagatai received territories near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> and either 4,000 or 8,000 subjects. He was known for his knowledge of Mongol law and custom, and chroniclers such as Juzjani noted the harshness of his temperament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With his brothers Jochi and Ögedei he commanded the right wing in the invasion of the Jin dynasty in 1211. In the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> in 1219 he was charged with building bridges and maintaining roads. He and Ögedei besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Otrar</a>, which fell in February 1220; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> killed or enslaved its entire population. At the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Gurganj</a>, which fell in April 1221, the usual narrative has Chagatai and Jochi quarrel over how to conduct it until Genghis placed Ögedei over both; the historian Christopher Atwood argues that this account was a later invention meant to support Ögedei's right to rule.</p><p>Genghis thought Chagatai too strict and narrow-minded for a ruler and was troubled by his hatred of Jochi, and excluded him from the succession. After Genghis died in 1227, Chagatai held to his father's will, prevented any usurpation before the accession of Ögedei in 1229, and presided over the coronation with Tolui and their uncle Temüge. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> his capital and summer pastures were at Almaliq. He disputed jurisdiction with the imperial official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-yalavach">Mahmud Yalavach</a>, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> revolted against tax demands in 1238 he left its suppression to Ögedei's armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Ögedei died in December 1241, Chagatai's support made Töregene regent. He died in 1242; his wife Yesülün accused a steward named Vajir of poisoning him and had him executed. His grandson Qara Hülegü succeeded him but was displaced by Chagatai's son Yesü-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> between 1246 and 1250.</p><p>Chagatai upheld traditional Mongol law, which forbade elements of Islamic practice, and gained a reputation for being anti-Muslim; one contemporary Muslim writer claimed that he urged Ögedei to kill every Muslim in the empire. The historians Michael Hope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-jackson">Peter Jackson</a> think this far from the truth, pointing to the powerful Muslim officials at his court. His descendants were nonetheless far slower to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> than the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chagatai Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chagatai Khan</b> (r. 1227–1242) received lands formerly belonging to the Qara Khitai and the Khwarazmian realm: Transoxiana (much of modern Uzbekistan), the Ili River valley, Kashgaria and the Tarim Basin, and the Semirechye steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in Almaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1234" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chagatai Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1234">What was here in 1234? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
